Cursor changed a lot this year. It was acquired by SpaceX, it is now sold as part of "The X Subscription" next to Grok and X, its cheapest paid tier only runs Cursor's own models, and in November it published a post-mortem on a supply-chain security incident. None of that makes it a bad editor. It does give a reasonable developer a reason to ask what else is out there, and "what else" now covers editors, terminal agents, IDE plugins and cloud agents, not just VS Code forks.

We checked ten real alternatives against their own pricing pages, docs and GitHub repos on November 27, 2026. The short version, and it is our judgment, not a benchmark result: Claude Code is our pick for agent-first work, GitHub Copilot is the lowest-friction switch, Zed suits people who want a native editor and their own agents, Kilo Code is the open-source pick, and Antigravity is the cheapest way to try agents. The rest depends on your editor, your budget and how much structure you want.

{% youtube id="8qeym0p969Y" title="Stop Using VS Code! Best AI Code Editors 2026 (Cursor vs Windsurf vs Zed)" /%}

*A third-party 2026 walkthrough of Cursor, Windsurf and Zed from The Macroscope (title confirmed through YouTube oEmbed on November 27, 2026). It is a reviewer's opinion, not a vendor demo, and it covers only three of the ten tools below.*

## What is Cursor, and why do people look for alternatives?

{% callout type="info" %}
**What is Cursor?** Cursor describes itself as "your coding agent for building ambitious software." It is an AI-first code editor and agent: you describe a task, the agent works across your project, and you review the changes. Its pricing docs also mention Cloud Agents, Automations, Bugbot (code review) and Origin (code hosting, in early beta). As of November 27, 2026 it is part of SpaceX (Cursor's own post is dated August 14, 2026) and is sold through The X Subscription.
{% /callout %}

**Cursor AI features** that people usually mean when they search for them: an Agent that edits across files, Cloud Agents and Automations, code review with Bugbot, a code-hosting product called Origin, MCP support (stdio, SSE and Streamable HTTP transports per its docs), and Cursor's own models (Grok 4.7, Grok 4.6, Grok 4.5 and Composer 2.5). In October it added Computer Use, which lets agents click and type across desktop apps.

{% x id="2113661518780998003" /%}

Reasons readers give for leaving, in the order we see them:

1. **Pricing changed shape.** Cursor's plans are now Free $0, Plus $8, Premium $30, Super $100 and Ultra $200 a month (Teams $30, $100 or $200 a seat). Plus has no third-party models, no Auto and no Cloud Agents. Premium and above bill third-party models at API list price from prepaid credit. The older Hobby, Start, Pro, Pro+ and Ultra plans are labelled "legacy" and keep working for existing subscribers. Source: cursor.com/pricing and Cursor's Models and Pricing docs, read November 27, 2026.
2. **Ownership.** The August 14 post says SpaceX completed its acquisition of Cursor, a process that began in April with a partnership with SpaceXAI. Some teams want a vendor with a different parent. Others see cheaper compute as a plus. We take no side.
3. **Security.** Cursor's November 13 post says an attacker obtained an access key that Fireworks had exposed, and on October 29 used a stolen credential to publish a malicious copy of Anthropic's Claude Code extension in Cursor's VS Code extension marketplace. Cursor also says less than 0.01% of customers had API credentials exposed and that it contacted affected customers directly. These are Cursor's own statements; we did not independently verify them.

{% x id="2121411147714970092" /%}

4. **Workflow preference.** Plenty of developers now prefer a terminal agent or a plugin inside the editor they already use.

If you are happy with Cursor, nothing here forces a move. For a head-to-head with the closest terminal rival, see our [Claude Code vs Cursor comparison](https://agentskills.codes/blog/claude-code-vs-cursor-2026). For a deeper pricing read on Cursor itself, our sister site AgentPedia has a [Cursor vs Antigravity breakdown](https://agentpedia.codes/blog/cursor-vs-antigravity).

## Quick picks

| If you want | Pick | Why, in one line |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The best agent quality and depth | Claude Code | Built around the agent loop; terminal, IDE, desktop and web |
| The easiest switch from Cursor | GitHub Copilot | Works in VS Code, Visual Studio, Xcode, JetBrains IDEs, Neovim and Eclipse |
| The closest editor-plus-agent experience | Windsurf (now Devin Desktop) | Editor with free Tab completions and an Agent Command Center |
| A native editor with your own agents | Zed | Parallel agent threads, bring your own keys or external agents |
| Best free way to try agents | Google Antigravity | Free individual plan with a weekly baseline quota |
| Best open source | Kilo Code | MIT-licensed, any model, VS Code, JetBrains and CLI |
| Spec-driven, structured work | Kiro | Specs and steering files in an IDE and CLI |
| JetBrains users | JetBrains Junie | Runs in IntelliJ-based IDEs and as a CLI, bring your own key |
| Already paying for ChatGPT | Codex | Included in ChatGPT plans, CLI is Apache-2.0 |
| A cheap, regional AI IDE | Trae | $20 Pro, $8 Lite in select countries, SOLO mode |

