- **Kiro**: the pricing page lists Kiro Web among the places a subscription works.
- **Junie**: a GitHub Action lets it respond to issues and pull requests in CI.

## Head-to-head: which to pick

### Claude Code vs Codex

Both are agent-first, both run in a terminal, and both reach into editors through extensions. Claude Code is sold through Claude Pro, Max and Ultra with no free tier. Codex is included in ChatGPT plans, including a free allowance subject to rollout, and its CLI is Apache-2.0 while the Claude Code repo lists no license. Pick Claude Code if you already pay for Claude and want its subagents and CLAUDE.md memory. Pick Codex if ChatGPT is already on your expense report and you want an open-source CLI you can script. We found no neutral benchmark that settles which agent writes better code, so run each on one real task from your own repo before you commit. We covered the Cursor side of this comparison in [Claude Code vs Cursor](https://agentskills.codes/blog/claude-code-vs-cursor-2026).

### GitHub Copilot vs Windsurf (Devin Desktop)

Copilot asks you to change nothing: it lives in the editor you already use and starts at $10. Windsurf gives you a full editor with unlimited Tab completions and inline edits on the free plan, and Pro is $20. Copilot's agent features draw down GitHub AI Credits, while Windsurf describes its quotas in words ("light," "significantly higher") rather than numbers. Pick Copilot if your team is mixed across VS Code, JetBrains and Visual Studio. Pick Windsurf if you want one editor that feels closest to Cursor and you are happy to move into it.

### Zed vs Kilo Code

Both are about choice. Zed is a native editor with an Agent Panel, bring-your-own-key use on a free plan, and external agents over ACP. Kilo Code is an MIT-licensed extension (plus a CLI) that works with many providers and local models. Pick Zed if you want to leave VS Code behind and run Claude Agent or Codex CLI in a first-party panel. Pick Kilo Code if you want to stay in VS Code or JetBrains and control exactly how you pay for inference. Note that the Kilo repo says `main` is not accepting new features and new work goes to `v2`, so expect changes.

